course: dictionary
lesson: 1515
title: Words for remembering
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what a place is for, even before you know their exact meaning. «A cenotaph is a stone war memorial in a town, where people gather for a minute's silence to remember those who died in wars.» The clues are stone, memorial, gather, silence and remember. Put them together and you can work out that a cenotaph is a place for remembering.
explain: Ask yourself what people do there. They gather, they keep quiet and they remember. That tells you the place is serious and respectful, not a place for play or fun. Swap your meaning into the sentence: «a place where people gather to remember» fits, so your guess is probably right.
explain: The word links to Remembrance Day. On that day, people lay wreaths of poppies at the cenotaph in London, and in Australia and New Zealand people remember their soldiers on Anzac Day in a similar way. Many towns have their own cenotaph with the names of local soldiers on it.
fact: A cenotaph is a monument built to remember people who died somewhere else and are buried elsewhere.
fact: A memorial is something built to help people remember a person or an event.
fact: «The Cenotaph» with a capital C is the famous war memorial in Whitehall, London.
fact: On Remembrance Day, people lay wreaths of poppies at the cenotaph and keep a silence.
fact: Context clues such as «silence» and «remember» show you what a word means, even when you do not know it yet.
exercise: «A cenotaph is a stone war memorial in a town, where people gather for a minute's silence.» What is a cenotaph? | A shop that sells stone | *A monument built to remember people who died somewhere else | A park where children play | A church with a tall tower
exercise: Find and copy one word from the sentence that tells you people are being quiet and respectful. | *silence | war | stone | town
exercise: What is «The Cenotaph» in London? | A busy railway station | A school for soldiers | A museum of old toys | *The famous war memorial in Whitehall
test: On Remembrance Day, what do people lay at the cenotaph? | Birthday cards | Sandcastles | *Wreaths of poppies | Kites
test: Why do people keep a minute's silence at a cenotaph? | To watch a parade of cars | *To remember those who died in wars | To sell tickets | To be rude and noisy
exam: Ruby says a cenotaph is a place for playing football. Why is she wrong? | Because football pitches are always made of stone | *Because people gather there quietly to remember the dead | Because a cenotaph is a type of shop | Because cenotaphs are only found in schools
